    void tryToSleep() {
        while(!asleep) {
            countSomeSheep();
        }
    }

    private void countSomeSheep() {
        // This method can be left empty or contain a simple note indicating the observing action.
        System.out.println("Observing fireflies...");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Scout scout = new Scout();
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3); // Ensure total threads used in the program doesn't exceed 3
        executor.submit(() -> { scout.tryToSleep(); }); // Submit scout to sleep task

        // Add some time for the scout to start observing fireflies...
        Thread.sleep(2000);
        scout.asleep = true;  // Interrupt the scout from sleeping

        executor.shutdown();  // Shut down the thread pool
    }
}
```
The Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3) creates a new thread pool with at most three threads. The first created thread is used by the main method, so only two additional threads can be used in this case. The ExecutorService.submit() method starts a new task to count fireflies. If more than two tasks are submitted, they'll wait until a running task finishes. 

The scout sleeps after two seconds (Thread.sleep(2000)) and then tries to wake up by setting the asleep variable to true. The main thread then shuts down the executor service, which prevents any new tasks from starting and waits for already submitted tasks to finish their execution.

